import "../../src/lib.neo"
type JournalPost={slug:Arr u8 _,title:Arr u8 _,category:Arr u8 _,minutes:Arr u8 _,date:Arr u8 _,author:Arr u8 _,initials:Arr u8 _,excerpt:Arr u8 _}
fn journal_post(index:i64)->JournalPost = if index==0 then JournalPost {slug="a-smaller-web",title="Make room for a smaller web",category="Engineering",minutes="7 min read",date="November 8, 2026",author="Maya Chen",initials="MC",excerpt="The best thing we can send to a browser is often a little less. On building useful experiences with fewer moving parts."} else if index==1 then JournalPost {slug="slower-mornings",title="The case for slower mornings",category="Design",minutes="5 min read",date="November 5, 2026",author="Sam Rivera",initials="SR",excerpt="A notebook, a window, and a bit of unclaimed time. Why a thoughtful start can change the rest of the day."} else JournalPost {slug="quiet-interfaces",title="A good interface gets out of the way",category="Notes",minutes="8 min read",date="October 28, 2026",author="Alex Morgan",initials="AM",excerpt="Clear labels, quiet defaults, and a little care at the edges. Notes on making software feel more human."}
fn journal_body(index:i64)->WebHtml = if index==0 then web_html("<section><h2>Start with something useful</h2><p>A good web page begins with a promise: there is something here worth your time. A story, an answer, a tool. That promise should arrive before a progress indicator, before a bundle of code, and before the browser has to assemble the page itself.</p></section><section><h2>The first response matters</h2><p>Server rendering makes that first response useful. The title is already a title. Links are already links. The browser can lay out the document while the rest of the page arrives. Adding a small interaction later does not have to turn the whole document into an application.</p></section><section><h2>A little restraint travels a long way</h2><p>We often measure the cost of a feature in lines of code. Our readers pay a different cost: bytes, waiting, memory, and battery. Keeping those costs visible changes the decisions we make. A filter can be a GET form. A chart can be an SVG. An article can simply be HTML.</p></section><section><h2>Leave room to grow</h2><p>This is not a call to make every site identical. It is an invitation to start with the smallest useful shape, measure what actually happens, and add complexity where it earns its place. The result is a web that feels a little more immediate, and a little more welcoming.</p></section>") else if index==1 then web_html("<section><h2>Before the first notification</h2><p>There is a small pocket of time before the day has decided what it wants from us. It can disappear into a screen, or it can become something else: a walk, a page in a notebook, a few minutes looking out of a window.</p></section><section><h2>Make a little space</h2><p>Designing a morning is less about finding an ideal routine and more about removing one source of friction. Put the notebook where you will see it. Leave a question unfinished the night before. Give yourself a place to begin that does not require a decision.</p></section><section><h2>Attention is a material</h2><p>The quality of our work often follows the quality of our attention. Like any material, attention has limits. It bends under constant interruption. It becomes more useful when we leave enough space around the thing we are trying to understand.</p></section><section><h2>Something to return to</h2><p>Not every morning will be quiet. That is fine. A good practice is a place to return to, not another standard to fall short of. Start small. Notice what changes. Keep the part that helps.</p></section>") else web_html("<section><h2>Help people move forward</h2><p>Most people do not arrive at an interface hoping to admire it. They want to finish something. The best design gives them a clear next step, enough context to feel confident, and a way back if they change their mind.</p></section><section><h2>Good defaults are generous</h2><p>A thoughtful default saves a decision without taking away control. It is the sensible date range, the helpful empty state, the form that remembers what was entered. These details are small individually, but together they make a product feel considerate.</p></section><section><h2>The edges are the experience</h2><p>An error is not an exception to the design. Neither is a slow connection, a keyboard, a narrow screen, or a long name. These are ordinary conditions. Designing for them makes the central experience stronger too.</p></section><section><h2>Quiet does not mean empty</h2><p>An interface can be expressive without asking for constant attention. Typography can carry hierarchy. Color can point to an action. Motion can explain a change. The aim is not to remove character, but to let it support the work.</p></section>")
